+# Decision: Visit Concurrency Policy
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers submit visit records from tablets that can be offline for up to 48 hours. Two workers may independently correct the same visit during that window, producing two complete edits that arrive at the server without either worker knowing the other edited the same record. The server must not treat arrival order as evidence of authority.
+
+The concurrency policy determines what the server keeps, what it puts in front of coordinators, and what tablet-sync and review-export must be designed around. A wrong choice here requires reworking all three downstream nodes.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gate — No silent discard.** A submitted edit may never be silently overwritten or dropped. Any option that produces a reconciled record by discarding a submitted value without coordinator awareness is eliminated. This is a standing HLD tenet.
+
+**Scientific interpretability (major).** Tenet: *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable.* A coordinator and the science partner must be able to trace any exported field value back to an identified submission by an identified worker. Auto-combination that produces a record no individual worker submitted breaks this traceability for the combined fields.
+
+**Scientific relationship preservation (major).** Visit fields are not independent scientific measurements. Species identification and cover estimate are one observation: changing the identification while keeping the prior cover estimate, or vice versa, creates a record that misrepresents both values. The server cannot infer cross-field relationships from field names alone. An option that combines fields automatically will silently create scientifically inconsistent records in those cases.
+
+**Coordinator review volume (moderate).** Fewer visits requiring coordinator resolution means faster path to Friday export and lower weekly workload for two coordinators. Edits that touch genuinely unrelated fields — correcting an observer name while another worker added a missing species — do not need human resolution if the system can safely combine them.
+
+**Implementation and maintenance burden (moderate).** Rules written today must be maintained as the visit schema evolves. Merge rules for structured fields (observer lists, per-species entries) interact with each other; an error in one rule can produce a silently wrong record rather than a visible conflict.
+
+**Coordinator cognitive model (minor).** Two non-technical coordinators will operate the review queue without a dedicated administrator. The review workflow's complexity should match what two people can learn and keep current.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Each submission is a complete replacement of the prior visit state, tagged with the revision ID of the state the worker edited from (the base revision). When the base matches the server's current HEAD, the submission advances the HEAD sequentially. When the base does not match — two workers edited from the same starting state — both complete revisions are preserved as competing revisions and the visit is marked needs-review. A coordinator reviews the full payloads side by side and selects one as canonical. The selection is recorded with the coordinator's identity, all competing revision IDs, and the timestamp.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A and worker B each fetch visit V at revision R1. Worker A submits with base R1; server advances HEAD to R2. Worker B submits with base R1; base ≠ HEAD (R2), so both R2 and B's submission are competing revisions. Visit V is needs-review. Coordinator sees R2 and B's full payloads, selects R2, records the resolution.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- *No silent discard* — **passes.** All competing revisions are retained permanently; coordinator explicitly selects one.
+- *Scientific interpretability* — **strong.** The exported record is exactly one worker's submitted payload, unchanged. The coordinator chose it from an identified set; every exported field traces directly to an identified submission.
+- *Scientific relationship preservation* — **strong.** No automatic field combination occurs. Every exported record is one worker's complete observation; cross-field consistency is the worker's own.
+- *Coordinator review volume* — **weak.** Any two independent edits to the same visit, even to unrelated fields, create review work. A team with 24 workers and frequent corrections to the same plots will see a larger queue than field-aware merge would produce.
+- *Implementation and maintenance burden* — **strong.** No merge rules. Revision log is append-only; conflict detection is a single equality check (base = HEAD?). Schema evolution does not affect the concurrency mechanism.
+- *Coordinator cognitive model* — **strong.** Coordinators choose between complete, self-consistent visit payloads. The comparison surface is the same fields they already understand; no field-level diff literacy is required.
+
+**Summary.** Larger review queue; simpler implementation; every exported record is traceable to a single identifiable submission.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Each submission includes its base state (a full snapshot of the visit when the worker began editing) and the resulting state. The server computes the field-level diff between each submission and its base. When two submissions change disjoint fields, the changes are combined automatically. When they change the same field, the overlap is flagged for coordinator review at the field level. A maintained set of merge rules governs how each field type is combined: scalars (last-write-wins, or flag), ordered lists such as the observer roster, and nested objects such as per-species entries.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A corrects observer names; worker B adds a missing species entry. Field diff: disjoint. Server combines automatically. Exported record contains A's observer correction and B's species addition, attributed separately. If both had changed the same species' cover estimate, that field alone would be flagged for coordinator review.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- *No silent discard* — **passes.** Auto-combined records are derived from both submissions; no submitted value is dropped without coordinator action on an overlap.
+- *Scientific interpretability* — **partial.** Auto-combined fields have per-field attribution (worker A contributed observer names, worker B contributed the species entry), but the exported record as a whole is not any single worker's submission. The chain of custody is at field level, not at record level.
+- *Scientific relationship preservation* — **weak.** Merge rules operate on field names, not on scientific meaning. A worker who changed a species identification from A to B and adjusted its cover estimate accordingly will have only the identification change auto-merged if the other worker did not touch cover estimates — unless the merge rules explicitly couple those fields. Maintaining all scientifically coupled field pairs requires domain knowledge the development team does not have and the coordinators would need to provide, review, and keep current as the field schema evolves.
+- *Coordinator review volume* — **strong.** Edits to non-overlapping fields — the most common independent-correction pattern — never reach the queue. Review work is proportional to actual field-level conflicts, not to the count of visits with any independent edit.
+- *Implementation and maintenance burden* — **weak.** Merge rules must be written for each field type (scalar, list, nested object) and maintained across schema changes. Rules interact; a rule that correctly handles observer lists may interact incorrectly with species entries. An error produces a silently wrong combined record rather than a visible conflict.
+- *Coordinator cognitive model* — **moderate.** Coordinators resolve field-level conflicts rather than choosing between whole visits. This requires understanding which fields are in scope for each conflict and what a correct resolution looks like at the field level.
+
+**Summary.** Smaller review queue; ongoing merge-rule maintenance; auto-combined records are traceable by field, not by whole submission.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The scientific interpretability and scientific relationship preservation criteria — both major — decide this. The science partner receives a CSV whose values must trace to identified field observations. Under whole-visit revisions, every exported field value traces to one worker's submission, chosen explicitly by a coordinator. Under field-aware merge, auto-combined records have field-level attribution but the record as a whole is a server artifact, not an observation. More critically, the merge rules would need to encode scientific coupling between fields (species identification and cover estimate being the clearest example) that the server cannot infer from field names. The team cannot maintain rules that represent scientific judgment they do not hold.
+
+The coordinator review volume criterion argues for field-aware merge, and it is a real cost: two workers correcting the same visit for unrelated reasons will generate review work that whole-visit revisions cannot avoid. The team accepts this tradeoff. A larger review queue is a visible, manageable operational cost; a silently miscombined scientific record may not surface until well after export.
+
+This decision turns on the tenet *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* and, by implication, the non-goal *No automated botanical validation or inference of scientific intent* — the HLD explicitly declines to have the system reason about scientific meaning, and field-aware merge would require exactly that to be reliable.
+
+**What this forecloses.** Field-level diffing and automatic combination at the record-store layer. Any future move toward automatic field merging requires revisiting this decision and the scientific-coupling problem it raises.
+
+**What it obligates downstream.**
+
+*Tablet-sync* must retain the current revision ID for each visit on the tablet and include it as the base revision ID in every submission. When a tablet receives a needs-review response, it must surface this to the field worker so they understand their submission was accepted but is under coordinator review — not lost. Tablets that lose local state (wipe, reinstall) have no stored revision ID; the handling of a first-submission from a state-lost tablet is an open question for the tablet-sync design to resolve.
+
+*Review-export* must provide a revision comparison screen showing complete payloads of all competing revisions side by side, identified by worker and receipt time, with a coordinator action to select one. It must exclude needs-review visits from every export run and provide a count of unresolved visits so coordinators can assess the queue before each Friday export.
